# Liebermann, Gesetze I p. 627: ECf and ECf retr, Inscr., Prol., Prol. 1
FN_A = ('<i>Vgl. mein</i> Über Leges Edwardi Conf. <i>Halle</i> 1896. — <i>Verfasser steht der Sprache, Literatur und Verfassung der Angelsachsen ferner und hierarchischem Anspruch des</i> 12. <i>Jhs. näher als</i> In. Cn., Cons. Cn. <i>oder</i> Quadr. <i>und</i> Hn. <i>Er kennt den Wechsel des Besitzes an gewissen Gütern um</i> 1093 <i>bis</i> 1115 <i>oder</i> 1130; <i>er grollt über ein Ereigniss von</i> 1096, <i>wohl als selbsterlebt; Stephans Zugeständnisse</i> 1135/36 <i>benutzt er nicht. Er kennt vielleicht Warwickshire.</i> '
        '<i>Hss.:</i> Rl <i>um</i> 1190; Hk (<i>wovon</i> Ed 2 <i>abhängt</i>) Cl Hr spl (<i>Vorlage für</i> Pl <i>und</i> S, <i>dem</i> Vl <i>folgt</i>) <i>und die</i> 34, 1a <i>endende Classe</i> adow, <i>der zugehören</i> Ad (<i>um</i> 1210) Do No Ck <i>und</i> Wo, <i>die Vorlage für</i> Ed 1. '
        '<i>Der Retractator, jüngerer Zeitgenoss jenes, schreibt</i> (<i>vielleicht in Kent</i>) <i>vor</i> 1159. — <i>Sein Werk steht nur in der</i> Tripartita [<i>d. h. hinter</i> Wl art <i>und vor der</i> Genealogia Normann., <i>zwei Werken anderer Verfasser. Die</i> Genealogia, <i>von</i> 1136—54 (<i>edirt von mir</i> Über Ed. Conf. <i>S.</i> 134) <i>stammt vielleicht vom Tripartita-Sammler.</i>] <i>Hss.:</i> Cb <i>um</i> 1160, Dc Sp Oh Lm Wi Ar pehy, <i>die Vorlage von</i> Pe Hy; <i>ferner die Hss. mit den Überschriften des</i> Rubricator II, <i>nach</i> 1154, <i>nämlich</i> Ld 1 Cu Hv L Db Hunt-Anhang (<i>dem zugehören</i> Lb <i>um</i> 1200, rv [<i>Vorlage von</i> Ra Vi; <i>vgl.</i> Wl art. Inscr.¹] Gr Rc Va La Ba Ph Kn <i>und</i> Lond, <i>d. i.</i> Leges Anglorum s. XIII in. Lond. coll. [<i>vgl. deren Edition von mir S.</i> 38—64] <i>in</i> K Co (<i>woraus</i> Ld 2 <i>schöpfte</i>) Or <i>und</i> Ce, <i>d. i. einer Lichfielder Chronik</i> 14. <i>Jhs.</i> — <i>Aus</i> ECf. <i>und</i> ECf. retr. <i>mengt den Text</i> Ce, <i>womit in Londons Guildhall</i> Horn <i>und</i> Liber memor. (<i>vgl. S.</i> 633¹⁰) <i>stimmen. Überschriften des</i> Rubricator II <i>setzt zu</i> ECf. <i>Classe</i> spl.',
        'Cf. my Über Leges Edwardi Conf., Halle 1896. — The author stands further from the language, literature and constitution of the Anglo-Saxons, and closer to the hierarchical claims of the 12th century, than In. Cn., Cons. Cn. or Quadr. and Hn. He knows of the change of ownership of certain estates around 1093 to 1115 or 1130; he is aggrieved at an event of 1096, probably as one he lived through; he does not use Stephen’s concessions of 1135/36. He perhaps knows Warwickshire. '
        'MSS: Rl c. 1190; Hk (on which Ed 2 depends) Cl Hr spl (the exemplar of Pl and S, which Vl follows) and the class adow, which ends at 34, 1a, to which belong Ad (c. 1210) Do No Ck and Wo, the exemplar of Ed 1. '
        'The Retractator, a younger contemporary of that author, writes (perhaps in Kent) before 1159. — His work stands only in the Tripartita [i.e. after Wl art and before the Genealogia Normann., two works by other authors. The Genealogia, of 1136–54 (edited by me, Über Ed. Conf. p. 134), perhaps comes from the compiler of the Tripartita.] MSS: Cb c. 1160, Dc Sp Oh Lm Wi Ar pehy, the exemplar of Pe Hy; further the MSS with the headings of Rubricator II, after 1154, namely Ld 1 Cu Hv L Db Hunt-Anhang (to which belong Lb c. 1200, rv [the exemplar of Ra Vi; cf. Wl art. Inscr.¹] Gr Rc Va La Ba Ph Kn and Lond, i.e. Leges Anglorum s. XIII in. Lond. coll. [cf. my edition of it, pp. 38–64] in K Co (from which Ld 2 drew) Or and Ce, i.e. a Lichfield chronicle of the 14th century. — Ce mixes its text from ECf. and ECf. retr.; London’s Guildhall Horn and Liber memor. (cf. p. 633¹⁰) agree with it. Class spl adds the headings of Rubricator II to ECf.')
FN_B = ('<i>so betitelt seit</i> 17. <i>Jh., nicht original; Überschr. fehlt</i> Rl Hk; <i>die originale Inscriptio nannte</i> Wilhelm I. (<i>und vielleicht, wie in</i> ECf retr., Eadward); Incipit [<i>fehlt</i> Wo] tractatus de legibus et consuetudinibus Anglie editis [-te <i>Ed; fehlt</i> Do] tempore magni regis Willelmi <i>Ad Do Wo No Ck Ed;</i> De legibus regis Willelmi <i>Hr;</i> Incipit lex gloriosi Anglorum regis Ædwardi [Ed. <i>S</i>] <i>S Pl;</i> Hee sunt leges Edwardi regis <i>Cl</i>',
        'titled so since the 17th century, not original; the heading is lacking in Rl Hk; the original Inscriptio named William I (and perhaps, as in ECf retr., Edward); Incipit [lacking in Wo] tractatus de legibus et consuetudinibus Anglie editis [-te Ed; lacking in Do] tempore magni regis Willelmi Ad Do Wo No Ck Ed; De legibus regis Willelmi Hr; Incipit lex gloriosi Anglorum regis Ædwardi [Ed. S] S Pl; Hee sunt leges Edwardi regis Cl')
FN_STAR = ('<i>hier fügt zu</i> Ce, <i>vergeblich</i> Lond, <i>u.</i> 34, 1a, <i>zu bessern versuchend, und</i> (<i>da</i> Ealdred 1069 <i>starb,</i> Hugo <i>erst</i> 1075 <i>geweiht ward</i>) <i>in sich widersprechend:</i> Aldredus autem Eboracensis archiepiscopus, qui regem Willelmum coronauerat, et Hugo Londoniensis episcopus per preceptum regis scripserunt propriis manibus omnia que predicti iurati dixerunt.',
           'here Ce adds, trying in vain to improve on Lond (below, 34, 1a), and contradicting itself (since Ealdred died in 1069 and Hugh was only consecrated in 1075): Aldredus autem Eboracensis archiepiscopus, qui regem Willelmum coronauerat, et Hugo Londoniensis episcopus per preceptum regis scripserunt propriis manibus omnia que predicti iurati dixerunt.')
